First, find all \( x \) such that \( g(x) = 3 \) by finding the intersection points with \( y = 3 \).

[asy]
import graph; size(9cm);

real lsf=0.5;

pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10);

defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black;

real xmin=-3.5,xmax=3.5,ymin=-0.5,ymax=5.5;

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("$x$",xmin,xmax,defaultpen+black,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,OmitTick(0)),Arrows(6),above=true);
yaxis("$y$",ymin,ymax,defaultpen+black,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,OmitTick(0)),Arrows(6),above=true);

real g(real x) { if (x < -1) return -0.5*x^2 + x + 4; else if (x < 2) return -x + 3; else return x - 2; }
draw(graph(g,-3,-1));
draw((-1,3.5)--(2,1));
draw(graph(g,2,3));

draw((-3,3)--(3,3),red+linewidth(0.5));

label("$g(x)$",(-2.5,5),E);
label("$y = 3$", (3,3), red);

dot("$(-3,3.5)$", (-3,3.5), NW);
dot("$(-1,3.5)$", (-1,3.5), NE);
dot("$(0,3)$", (0,3), N);
dot("$(2,1)$", (2,1), S);
dot("$(3,1)$", (3,1), SE);

[/asy]

From the graph, \( g(x) = 3 \) for \( x = 0 \).  If \( g(g(x)) = 3 \), then \( g(x) = 0 \). Check \( g(x) = 0 \).

[asy]
import graph; size(9cm);
real g(real x) { if (x < -1) return -0.5*x^2 + x + 4; else if (x < 2) return -x + 3; else return x - 2; }
draw(graph(g,-3,3));
dot("$(0,3)$", (0,3), N);

[/asy]

By tracing \( y = g(x) \) against \( y = 0 \), we see it intersects at \( x = 2 \). Therefore, \( g(2) = 0 \), and \( g(g(2)) = g(0) = 3 \).

Therefore, the solution to \( g(g(x)) = 3 \) is $x = 2$. The answer is: $$\boxed{1 \text{ solution}}$$

